title: BunnyMark
intro: A benchmark: how many bouncing bunnies this computer keeps up at sixty frames a second.
project: BunnyMark | Bunnies

chapter: The benchmark | Bunnies, and a script that counts them
select: BunnyMark
explain: component:BunnyMark | The BunnyMark script | It adds bunnies while the game keeps up sixty frames a second. When the game falls behind, it takes the last lot away and tries fewer, until it has the count.
explain: field:BunnyMark.Growth | Growth | Each step adds half as many bunnies again, and the step halves every time the game falls behind.
explain: field:BunnyMark.Precision | Precision | The run ends once a step would add less than this. For a quick look, a rougher count will do.
set: BunnyMark.Precision = 0.2
set: BunnyMark.Window = 0.5

chapter: A run | Counting bunnies
play
wait: 3
explain: game | Bunnies pour in | Each bunny is a sprite and a short script, copied from a prefab: an ordinary entity, just like a game's. The frame rate and the count show in the corner.
wait: 3
explain: game | Big scenes | The scene keeps its sprites and scripts by type, so each frame finds them without going through every bunny again and again, and allocates nothing as it goes. In games with physics, each body only tests what is near it.
wait: 4
explain: game | The count | When it has found the count, it shows it along the bottom, and saves it with the best so far.
tap: Space
wait: 2
explain: game | Again | Space starts a new run.
stop
say: Exported games are measured the same way: a script in the tools folder builds a game from this template, plays it, and prints the count, so changes to the engine can say how much faster they made it.
